# The built-in customisation is exact bytes. It is embedded in katana_cad with
# #embed, and a customisation a user keeps records a digest of the bytes it was
# made from (entity::customisationDigest), by which the program tells one
# edition of the built-in from another. A checkout that rewrote its line ends
# (core.autocrlf on Windows) would give a program with another digest than the
# same commit built elsewhere, and every kept copy would be reported as made
# from another edition.
resources/customisation/*.json -text

# The licence text is exact bytes, as the Free Software Foundation publishes it.
# A checkout that rewrote its line ends (core.autocrlf on Windows) would no
# longer hold the unmodified text, so it is not converted.
LICENSE -text

# A shell script is run by bash, on Windows too (tools/build_visible.sh in Git
# Bash), and bash reads a carriage return as part of each line: a checkout that
# gave it CRLF line ends (core.autocrlf on Windows) breaks every line.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The Rust port (docs/rust.md) is edited on Windows and built in WSL from this
# one checkout, and is written from both sides. LF in every checkout, as the
# shell scripts are, keeps the bytes the same on both sides. (Not for string
# literals' sake: rustc reads a CRLF in a source file as LF.)
*.rs text eol=lf
